1. Column-Oriented NoSQL Databases

Column-oriented NoSQL databases store data in column families rather than fixed rows and columns like RDBMS.

Difference from RDBMS:

RDBMS has fixed tables, rows, and columns.
Column-family databases group related columns into column families.
Different rows can have different columns.
They are designed for distributed and large-scale data.

Advantages:

High scalability.
Fast read/write for large datasets.
Flexible schema.
High availability and fault tolerance.
Suitable for big-data applications.

Limitations:

Complex query support compared with SQL databases.
Data modeling is query-dependent.
Joins and multi-row transactions are limited.
Poor design can cause performance problems.
2. HBase vs Cassandra
Feature	HBase	Cassandra
Data Model	Column-family	Wide-column
Architecture	Master + RegionServers	Peer-to-peer
Consistency	Stronger consistency	Tunable consistency
Availability	High	Very high
Scalability	Horizontal	Horizontal
Fault Tolerance	HDFS replication	Data replication
Query	Get, Put, Scan, Filter	CQL
Best Use	Hadoop/big-data systems	Highly distributed applications

Choice: For a highly distributed application, Cassandra is generally preferred because it has a peer-to-peer architecture, no single master, high availability, and excellent multi-datacenter support.

3. Apache HBase Architecture

Main components:

HMaster: Manages regions, load balancing, and RegionServers.
RegionServer: Handles read/write requests.
ZooKeeper: Provides coordination and tracks server status.
HDFS: Provides distributed and fault-tolerant storage.
Region: A horizontal portion of an HBase table.
MemStore: Stores recent writes in memory before flushing to HFiles.

Write operation:

Client contacts HMaster/ZooKeeper to locate the RegionServer.
Client sends data to the RegionServer.
Data is written to the WAL and MemStore.
MemStore is eventually flushed to HFiles in HDFS.

Read operation:

Client locates the required Region.
RegionServer checks MemStore and block cache.
If necessary, it reads HFiles from HDFS.
Results are returned to the client.

